WEBVTT - Most Fascinating Stories - Episode 272: Malcolm X: Detroit Red, the Nation of Islam, the Hajj to Mecca, and Human Rights

1
00:00:03.040 --> 00:00:05.600
[Rebecca] Welcome to Most Fascinating Stories.

2
00:00:05.600 --> 00:00:06.880
[Rebecca] I'm Rebecca.

3
00:00:06.880 --> 00:00:15.680
[Rebecca] Today, our top 50 famous non-president American series explores the fiery intellectual colossus of black pride and human dignity.

4
00:00:15.680 --> 00:00:22.480
[Rebecca] The street hustler who transformed himself in a prison cell into a global revolutionary champion of human rights.

5
00:00:22.480 --> 00:00:25.600
[Rebecca] Here is George with the life of Malcolm X.

6
00:00:28.000 --> 00:00:35.920
[George] Born Malcolm Little in May 1925 in Omaha, Nebraska, Malcolm was born into the shadow of racial terror.

7
00:00:35.920 --> 00:00:44.880
[George] His father, Earl Little, was an outspoken Baptist preacher and organizer for Marcus Garvey's Universal Negro Improvement Association.

8
00:00:44.880 --> 00:00:49.920
[George] His Grenadaborn mother Louise was an articulate recording secretary for the movement.

9
00:00:49.920 --> 00:01:04.000
[George] Before Malcolm was born, armed Ku Klux Klansmen on horseback surrounded their Omaha home, shattering windows with rifles, moving to Lansing, Michigan, their house was burned to the ground by white supremacist night riders.

10
00:01:04.000 --> 00:01:18.240
[George] In 1931, when Malcolm was six years old, his father's battered body was found severed beneath a streetcar, an apparent white supremacist murder that local police ruled a suicide to deny the family insurance benefits.

11
00:01:18.240 --> 00:01:28.960
[George] Crushed by poverty, his mother suffered an emotional breakdown and was committed to a state psychiatric institution, scattering Malcolm and his siblings into foster homes.

12
00:01:28.960 --> 00:01:41.280
[George] Despite graduating top of his junior high class in Mason, Michigan, his dream of becoming a lawyer was crushed when an English teacher told him coldly, a lawyer, that's no realistic goal for a nigger.

13
00:01:41.280 --> 00:01:44.320
[George] You need to think about something you can do with your hands.

14
00:01:44.320 --> 00:01:45.840
[George] Like carpentry.

15
00:01:45.840 --> 00:01:59.920
[George] Bitterly disillusioned, Teenage Malcolm moved to Boston's Roxbury neighborhood and Harlem, adopting the street alias Detroit Red, falling into an underworld life of zoot suits, conked hair, numbers running, burglary and narcissism.

16
00:02:01.080 --> 00:02:08.440
[George] Which ended in February 1946 when he was sentenced to ten years in Massachusetts State Prison at age 20.

17
00:02:08.440 --> 00:02:16.120
[George] Born Malcolm Little in Omaha, Nebraska, in May 1925, his childhood was engulfed in racial terror.

18
00:02:16.120 --> 00:02:24.520
[George] Ku Klux Klan Knightriders surrounded their home, and when the family relocated to Michigan, white vigilantes burned their home to the ground.

19
00:02:24.520 --> 00:02:34.760
[George] In 1931, his father Earl Little, a Baptist preacher and outspoken follower of Marcus Garvey, was murdered and placed across streetcar tracks.

20
00:02:34.760 --> 00:02:49.000
[George] Sent to a foster home after his mother suffered an emotional collapse, Malcolm was a brilliant student who dreamed of becoming a lawyer, only to have his white eighth-grade teacher dismissively tell him that law was no realistic goal for a black boy.

21
00:02:49.000 --> 00:02:58.760
[George] Drifting to Boston and Harlem, Malcolm adopted Zoot suits and street hustling as Detroit Red, leading to his arrest for burglary in 1946.

22
00:02:58.760 --> 00:03:11.960
[George] Inside the grim, granite walls of Charlestown State Prison and the Norfolk Prison Colony, 21-year-old Malcolm underwent one of the most remarkable intellectual and spiritual resurrections in American history.

23
00:03:11.960 --> 00:03:16.120
[George] Introduced by fellow inmate John Bembry to the prison library.

24
00:03:16.120 --> 00:03:19.240
[George] Malcolm recognized the limits of his street slang.

25
00:03:19.240 --> 00:03:29.560
[George] He obtained a dictionary and painstakingly hand copied every single word, definition, and punctuation mark from the first page of A to the final page of Z.

26
00:03:29.560 --> 00:03:34.120
[George] Reading late into the night by the faint corridor light outside his cell door.

27
00:03:34.120 --> 00:03:41.960
[George] He consumed classics of world history, philosophy, biology, and religion, reading Spinoza, Kant, W.E.B.

28
00:03:41.960 --> 00:03:45.680
[George] Du Bois, and the Atrocities of the Transatlantic Slave trade.

29
00:03:46.000 --> 00:04:04.240
[George] Simultaneously, his siblings introduced him to the teachings of Elijah Mohammed, leader of the nation of Islam, who preached that white supremacy was a demonic aberration and that black people must reclaim their ancestral dignity, embrace strict moral purity, and build economic independence.

30
00:04:04.240 --> 00:04:15.040
[George] Converted completely, Malcolm dropped his slave surname Little, adopting the letter X to signify the true African tribal name and heritage stolen by chattel slavery.

31
00:04:15.040 --> 00:04:27.280
[George] Sentenced to ten years at Charlestown State Prison, Malcolm was transferred to the progressive Norfolk prison colony, where an extensive library transformed his existence into a crucible of self-educ.

32
00:04:27.520 --> 00:04:42.720
[George] Copying every single word, definition, and punctuation mark from the dictionary from a to Z to expand his vocabulary, Malcolm devoured books on world history, philosophy, and linguistics by the dim glow of his cell door corridor light.

33
00:04:42.720 --> 00:04:58.560
[George] Introduced by his siblings to Elijah Mohammed's Nation of Islam, Malcolm embraced its message of black self-reliance and spiritual rebirth, casting aside his slave surname Little in favor of X to signify his lost African ancestral tribal name.

34
00:04:58.560 --> 00:05:10.320
[George] Released on parole in August 1952, 27-year-old Malcolm X rose with meteoric speed to become the premier national spokesman and chief evangelist of the nation of Islam.

35
00:05:10.320 --> 00:05:12.000
[George] Assigned to Temple No.

36
00:05:12.240 --> 00:05:26.560
[George] 7 in Harlem, Malcolm's razor sharp intellect, commanding 6'4 presence, and hypnotic oratorical brilliance electrified urban black communities, expanding the nation's membership from a few hundred to over 50,000.

37
00:05:26.560 --> 00:05:40.600
[George] Malcolm established the national newspaper Mohammed Speaks, opened mosques in every major American city, and converted charismatic champions like heavyweight boxing icon Cassius Clay, who took the name Mohammed Ali.

38
00:05:40.600 --> 00:05:53.080
[George] In his fiery speeches, Malcolm challenged the nonviolent philosophy of the mainstream civil rights movement, arguing that nonviolence in the face of violent police dogs and lynching was criminal suicide.

39
00:05:53.080 --> 00:05:56.200
[George] I don't call it violence when it's in self-defense.

40
00:05:56.200 --> 00:05:58.040
[George] I call it intelligence.

41
00:05:58.040 --> 00:06:09.640
[George] Malcolm fiercely ridiculed the goal of racial integration into an oppressive white society, demanding black economic self-sufficiency, separate institutions, and absolute cultural pride.

42
00:06:09.640 --> 00:06:11.800
[George] Appointed Minister of Temple No.

43
00:06:12.040 --> 00:06:23.800
[George] 7 in Harlem, Malcolm's razor-sharp intellect, fierce discipline, and electrifying oratory transformed the nation of Islam from a minor religious sect into a powerful national movement.

44
00:06:23.800 --> 00:06:33.320
[George] Malcolm established Mohammed Speaks, which grew into the largest circulating black newspaper in the country, and opened temples in dozens of major cities.

45
00:06:33.320 --> 00:06:50.360
[George] While mainstream civil rights leaders preached racial integration and nonviolence, Malcolm delivered an uncompromising indictment of systemic white racism, arguing that freedom, dignity, and self-defense were natural human rights that must be defended by any means necessary.

46
00:06:50.360 --> 00:07:00.680
[George] By the early 1960s, Malcolm's soaring international fame and media visibility ignited bitter jealousy among the nation of Islam's inner circle in Chicago.

47
00:07:00.680 --> 00:07:13.560
[George] In 1963, Malcolm discovered to his deep moral disillusionment that Elijah Mohammed had fathered children out of wedlock with several young teenage secretaries, violating the nation's strict moral laws.

48
00:07:13.560 --> 00:07:16.400
[George] Following the assassination of President John F.

49
00:07:16.640 --> 00:07:30.880
[George] Kennedy in November 1963, Malcolm responded to a reporter's question by stating that the murder was a case of the chickens coming home to roost, explaining that violence unleashed abroad had returned to strike the American head of state.

50
00:07:30.880 --> 00:07:36.480
[George] Seizing the public controversy, Elijah Mohammed silenced Malcolm for 90 days.

51
00:07:36.480 --> 00:07:48.320
[George] Recognizing that his life was in mortal danger from nation assassins, Malcolm announced his official break with the Nation of Islam on March 8, 1964, establishing Muslim Mosque, Inc.

52
00:07:48.640 --> 00:07:59.680
[George] And the secular organization of Afro American Unity, OAAU, to link the African American struggle to the broader global anti-colonial human rights movement in the United Nations.

53
00:07:59.680 --> 00:08:10.160
[George] Tensions within the Nation of Islam culminated in Malcolm's suspension in December 1963, following his blunt commentary on the assassination of President John F.

54
00:08:10.400 --> 00:08:14.640
[George] Kennedy, which he characterized as the chickens coming home to roost.

55
00:08:14.640 --> 00:08:27.760
[George] Discovering profound moral compromises within the nation's leadership, Malcolm formally broke with Elijah Mohammed in March 1964, founding Muslim Mosque, Inc., and the Organization of Afro American Unity.

56
00:08:27.760 --> 00:08:41.200
[George] In April 1964, delivering his masterwork Political Speech, The Ballot or the Bullet in Cleveland and Detroit, Malcolm urged African Americans to exercise political solidarity and voting power.

57
00:08:41.200 --> 00:08:47.840
[George] Warning that failure to achieve genuine democratic representation would inevitably lead to open rebellion.

58
00:08:47.840 --> 00:08:59.040
[George] In April 1964, Malcolm made a transformative journey that fundamentally revolutionized his worldview, making the Holy Hajj pilgrimage to Mecca in Saudi Arabia.

59
00:08:59.040 --> 00:09:10.280
[George] Standing before the sacred Kaaba alongside over a million Muslim pilgrims from every corner of the globe, Malcolm experienced an electrifying revelation of universal human brotherhood.

60
00:09:10.280 --> 00:09:25.160
[George] He saw Muslims of every skin color, blonde-haired, blue-eyed Europeans, dark-skinned Africans, Arabs and Asians, praying together, eating from the same plate, and sleeping side by side in mutual love and total equality.

61
00:09:25.160 --> 00:09:34.600
[George] Writing his famous letter from Mecca, Malcolm announced his conversion to Orthodox Sunni Islam, adopting the holy name El Hajj Malik El Shabbaz.

62
00:09:34.600 --> 00:09:40.120
[George] In the letter, he courageously disavowed his previous teaching that all white people were devils.

63
00:09:40.120 --> 00:09:52.520
[George] In the past I have permitted myself to be used to make sweeping indictments of all white people, the whole white race, and these generalizations have caused injuries to some whites who perhaps did not deserve to be hurt.

64
00:09:52.520 --> 00:10:00.360
[George] True Islam has shown me that a blanket indictment of all white people is as wrong as when whites make blanket indictments against blacks.

65
00:10:00.360 --> 00:10:06.280
[George] He returned to America preaching a broader, universal struggle for international human rights.

66
00:10:06.280 --> 00:10:16.200
[George] Embarking on the sacred Hajj pilgrimage to Mecca in April 1964, Malcolm experienced a profound spiritual epiphany that altered his worldview.

67
00:10:16.200 --> 00:10:31.240
[George] Walking alongside pilgrims of all races, embracing blonde haired, blue eyed fellow Muslims from Europe, alongside dark skinned believers from Africa and Asia, Malcolm witnessed genuine, unfeigned human brotherhood and spiritual unity.

68
00:10:31.240 --> 00:10:47.040
[George] Adopting the Muslim name El Hajj Malik el-Shabbaz, he wrote a historic letter from Mecca declaring that true Islam had eradicated racial prejudice from the hearts of its believers, leading him to abandon broad racial generalizations in favor of universal human justice.

69
00:10:47.360 --> 00:10:54.240
[George] In the final year of his life, Malcolm delivered his masterwork Political Address, The Ballot or the Bullet.

70
00:10:54.240 --> 00:11:03.120
[George] Speaking in Cleveland, Ohio, Malcolm declared that black Americans must use the ballot wisely to demand complete human dignity and economic power.

71
00:11:03.120 --> 00:11:09.760
[George] But warned that if the democratic system refused to grant equality, the only alternative would be the bullet.

72
00:11:09.760 --> 00:11:16.720
[George] It's time for us to submerge our differences and realize that it is best for us to first see that we have the same common problem.

73
00:11:16.720 --> 00:11:19.280
[George] We've got to fight until we overcome.

74
00:11:19.280 --> 00:11:37.280
[George] During trips across Africa, Malcolm met with heads of state including Kwame Nkrumah of Ghana, Gamal Abdel Nasser of Egypt, and Julius Nairer of Tanzania, lobbying African nations to bring formal charges of human rights violations against the United States and the United Nations.

75
00:11:37.280 --> 00:11:40.560
[George] But in New York, death stalked him every hour.

76
00:11:40.560 --> 00:11:48.560
[George] His home in East Elmhurst was firebombed in the middle of the night while his pregnant wife Betty Shabazz and four daughters slept inside.

77
00:11:48.560 --> 00:11:53.040
[George] Malcolm knew his days were numbered, calmly remarking to reporters.

78
00:11:55.680 --> 00:12:09.280
[George] Spending 24 weeks traveling across Africa and the Middle East, Malcolm met with revolutionary heads of state including Kwaim Nkrumah of Ghana, Gamal Abdel Nasser of Egypt, and Julius Nairer of Tanzania.

79
00:12:09.280 --> 00:12:27.280
[George] Addressing the organization of African Unity in Cairo, Malcolm urged African nations to bring the plight of 22 million African Americans before the Human Rights Commission of the United Nations, elevating the struggle from a domestic American civil rights dispute into an international human rights crusade.

80
00:12:27.280 --> 00:12:44.120
[George] On Sunday afternoon, February 21, 1965, 39-year-old Malcolm X stepped onto the stage of the Audubon Ballroom in Washington Heights, Manhattan, to address a crowd of 400 followers, including his wife Betty and young children.

81
00:12:44.120 --> 00:12:53.720
[George] As Malcolm uttered the traditional Islamic greeting, as Salamu Aleikam, peace be unto you, a stage disturbance broke out in the back of the hall.

82
00:12:53.720 --> 00:12:58.840
[George] As Malcolm raised his hands to restore calm, three gunmen rushed the stage.

83
00:12:58.840 --> 00:13:08.600
[George] One fired a sawed-off shotgun blast directly into Malcolm's chest at point blank range, while two others fired semi-automatic pistols into his fallen body.

84
00:13:08.600 --> 00:13:14.760
[George] Malcolm was rushed across the street to Columbia Presbyterian Hospital, where he was pronounced dead.

85
00:13:14.760 --> 00:13:21.720
[George] In his eulogy at Faith Temple Church in Harlem, actor and activist Aussie Davis delivered an immortal tribute.

86
00:13:21.720 --> 00:13:25.400
[George] Malcolm was our manhood, our living, black manhood.

87
00:13:25.400 --> 00:13:31.240
[George] This was his meaning to his people, and in honoring him, we honor the best in ourselves.

88
00:13:31.240 --> 00:13:41.640
[George] Collaborating with journalist Alex Haley, the autobiography of Malcolm X was published months after his death, becoming one of the most influential books in world history.

89
00:13:41.640 --> 00:13:46.040
[George] Malcolm X remains an eternal monument of revolutionary integrity.

90
00:13:46.040 --> 00:13:54.040
[George] The man who taught an oppressed people to stand tall, look the world in the eye, and demand respect and freedom by any means necessary.

91
00:13:54.040 --> 00:14:08.440
[George] Returning to New York aware that his life was in constant, imminent danger from both hostile intelligence agencies and former nation of Islam rivals, Malcolm refused to live in hiding, continuing his weekly Sunday afternoon rallies.

92
00:14:08.440 --> 00:14:24.240
[George] On February 21, 1965, at the Audubon Ballroom in Washington Heights, as he stepped to the wooden podium and uttered the Arabic greeting as Salamu Aleikum, three assassins rushed forward and opened fire with shotguns and pistols.

93
00:14:24.240 --> 00:14:34.080
[George] In his moving eulogy, actor Ossi Davis crowned him our own black shining prince, a man who never hesitated to tell America the unvarnished truth.

94
00:14:34.080 --> 00:14:46.880
[George] Malcolm X's uncompromising honesty, intellectual fearlessness, and relentless defense of human dignity cemented his legacy as one of the towering revolutionary figures of the modern world.

